To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function. This function is used to terminate the program and return an integer status to the operating system. The `exit()` function is declared in the `stdlib.h` library. Here is an example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
    exit(0);
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the program will print "Hello, World!" and then terminate, returning a status of 0 to the operating system. The status of 0 indicates that the program executed successfully.
